1
00:00:00,913 --> 00:00:03,933
Research papers are not written
to be read from start to finish,

2
00:00:03,953 --> 00:00:08,665
like a novel. So in this video, I will
show you the parts of a paper and the

3
00:00:08,685 --> 00:00:12,105
order to read them in. There's this
method I like, it's called a three-pass

4
00:00:12,125 --> 00:00:15,705
method. And that's the method I'm
going to be focusing on in this video.

5
00:00:15,725 --> 00:00:18,625
Most research papers are
built from the same parts.

6
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:22,905
And you need to know what each part is
for, so that you can stop reading it

7
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:27,725
like one big block of text. In a lot of
fields, the main body follows a pattern

8
00:00:27,745 --> 00:00:31,845
called IMRAD. It stands for
introduction, methods, results,

9
00:00:31,865 --> 00:00:35,365
and discussion. And around those,
you usually would see a title,

10
00:00:35,385 --> 00:00:40,545
an abstract, related work, background
section, a limitation section,

11
00:00:40,565 --> 00:00:43,105
a conclusion, and the references.

12
00:00:43,125 --> 00:00:45,905
The names will change a little
from field to field in HCI,

13
00:00:45,925 --> 00:00:50,105
which is my field. The results
section is often called findings.

14
00:00:50,125 --> 00:00:53,505
You don't need to read every part
of a research paper closely.

15
00:00:53,525 --> 00:00:55,865
There are three major parts that
do most of the work for you.

16
00:00:55,885 --> 00:00:59,885
The first one is the abstract. The
abstract is a short paragraph right under

17
00:00:59,905 --> 00:01:04,545
the title. It's the whole paper
squeezed into one paragraph.

18
00:01:04,565 --> 00:01:08,805
What they asked, what they did, what
they found, summarized in the abstract.

19
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:12,525
The second one is the method. The method
is where the authors tells you exactly

20
00:01:12,545 --> 00:01:15,945
what they did, who took part, how
many people, what they measure,

21
00:01:15,965 --> 00:01:19,605
how they measured it. This is the part
that tells you whether you can trust

22
00:01:19,625 --> 00:01:22,375
everything else in the paper. And the
third part is the discussion together

23
00:01:22,395 --> 00:01:26,605
with the limitation. The discussion is
where the authors explain what their

24
00:01:26,625 --> 00:01:31,625
results mean. And the limitation section
is where they say what their study

25
00:01:32,025 --> 00:01:35,725
cannot tell you. Like, hey, this
study does not provide this,

26
00:01:35,745 --> 00:01:38,345
something like that. And I think the
limitation is one of the most useful parts

27
00:01:38,365 --> 00:01:40,165
of a paper when you're
writing your thesis.

28
00:01:40,185 --> 00:01:43,705
For example, because it shows what is
still open and you can often find your

29
00:01:43,725 --> 00:01:46,925
research gap there. One of the best
known methods for reading research paper

30
00:01:46,945 --> 00:01:50,825
comes from a two-page paper called "How
to Read a Paper." It was written by

31
00:01:50,845 --> 00:01:51,965
S. Keshav.

32
00:01:53,213 --> 00:01:56,243
In his word, you should read
the paper in up to three passes,

33
00:01:56,263 --> 00:01:58,443
and each pass has a different job.

34
00:01:58,463 --> 00:02:02,263
The first pass tells you what the research
paper is about and whether you even

35
00:02:02,283 --> 00:02:06,843
need this paper at all. and the second
part tells you what the paper found and

36
00:02:06,863 --> 00:02:12,842
what evidence it has and the third part
is for the papers you need to understand

37
00:02:12,862 --> 00:02:17,722
completely. Pass one of Keshav's three-pass
method takes about five to ten minutes

38
00:02:17,742 --> 00:02:21,447
and it also gives you the
order to read the paper in.

39
00:02:21,526 --> 00:02:25,827
List it up in four steps. First you
carefully read the title of the paper,

40
00:02:25,847 --> 00:02:29,087
you read the abstract of the
paper, you read the introduction.

41
00:02:29,107 --> 00:02:33,047
Then second, you read the section
and the sub-section heading,

42
00:02:33,067 --> 00:02:36,506
but ignore every other thing. Then
third, you read the conclusion,

43
00:02:36,526 --> 00:02:38,987
and fourth, you glance
over the references.

44
00:02:39,007 --> 00:02:42,147
Just manually tick off the ones that
you have already read or you've already

45
00:02:42,167 --> 00:02:44,226
seen. If you watch my video
on how to read the book,

46
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:47,667
this is kind of the same idea
of reading the structure first,

47
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:49,967
same idea kind of applied
to research papers.

48
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:52,987
At the end of the first pass,
according to Keshav's method,

49
00:02:53,007 --> 00:02:56,726
you should be able to answer five
questions, and he calls them the five Cs.

50
00:02:56,746 --> 00:02:59,367
So when you've done the first
pass of reading a research paper,

51
00:02:59,387 --> 00:03:02,327
according to this method, you should be
able to answer these five questions.

52
00:03:02,347 --> 00:03:04,747
Category is what kind of
research paper is this?

53
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:06,587
Is it a study? Is it a new system?

54
00:03:06,607 --> 00:03:08,667
Is it a review of other papers?

55
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:12,643
Number two, context. What other
papers is this paper related to?

56
00:03:12,732 --> 00:03:16,751
Number three, correctness is
whether the assumptions seem valid.

57
00:03:16,771 --> 00:03:22,471
Number four, contribution is what the
paper has that was not in this subject

58
00:03:22,491 --> 00:03:26,411
of study before. And clarity is
whether the paper is well written.

59
00:03:26,431 --> 00:03:30,251
A lot of time after those five, ten
minutes you would find that maybe the paper

60
00:03:30,271 --> 00:03:32,232
is not relevant to what you're doing.

61
00:03:32,252 --> 00:03:35,372
At this point you stop, save
yourself a whole evening.

62
00:03:35,392 --> 00:03:37,772
You just do five, ten minutes to
save yourself a whole evening.

63
00:03:37,792 --> 00:03:40,163
You don't want to dedicate
three hours, four hours,

64
00:03:40,183 --> 00:03:43,803
five hours to reading a research paper
that is relevant to your own study.

65
00:03:43,823 --> 00:03:47,223
So the first pass is usually enough for
papers that are not in your research

66
00:03:47,243 --> 00:03:50,243
area but maybe they might matter
to you someday, who knows.

67
00:03:50,263 --> 00:03:54,723
Now pass two, the second pass of Keshav's
method for reading research paper

68
00:03:54,743 --> 00:03:58,103
takes up to an hour. After you've gone
through the first pass in five to ten

69
00:03:58,123 --> 00:04:01,183
minutes you decided that this
research paper is relevant to you,

70
00:04:01,203 --> 00:04:04,703
then you move on to reading the paper
with more care but you still ignore the

71
00:04:04,723 --> 00:04:08,223
detail. So you can ignore something
like a long table of statistics and you

72
00:04:08,243 --> 00:04:11,883
come back to that later. While
you read during the second pass.

73
00:04:11,903 --> 00:04:16,783
Keshav suggests writing down the key
point or making comments in the margin.

74
00:04:16,803 --> 00:04:19,783
So if you're not, you can
print out your research paper,

75
00:04:19,803 --> 00:04:22,623
this is something that I do now,
instead of just having it virtual,

76
00:04:22,643 --> 00:04:24,783
you could print it out
so you can mark it up.

77
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:27,623
Now this is the part where you
look properly at the figures,

78
00:04:27,643 --> 00:04:30,903
you look at the, you look at the figures,
the diagrams or the illustration in

79
00:04:30,923 --> 00:04:33,523
the paper, you know, pay some
attentions to the graph.

80
00:04:33,543 --> 00:04:36,443
And on the second pass, you mark some
of the references that you haven't read

81
00:04:36,463 --> 00:04:41,043
that looks relevant to you. That's how
one good research paper can lead you

82
00:04:41,063 --> 00:04:45,703
to another one. So by the time you're
at the end of the second pass,

83
00:04:45,723 --> 00:04:48,983
you should be able to explain the main
point of the paper and the evidence for

84
00:04:49,003 --> 00:04:52,963
it to someone else. Now, if you still
don't understand the research paper at

85
00:04:52,983 --> 00:04:56,423
this point, you can put it aside and
come back to it later after reading some

86
00:04:56,443 --> 00:04:59,183
background, or you can
move to the third pass.

87
00:04:59,203 --> 00:05:02,763
The third pass of Keshav's method for
reading research papers is for papers

88
00:05:02,783 --> 00:05:04,883
that you need to understand completely.

89
00:05:04,903 --> 00:05:07,743
The ones that, for example,
your thesis is built on.

90
00:05:07,763 --> 00:05:12,683
Keshav writes that the key to the third pass
is to attempt to virtually re-implement

91
00:05:12,703 --> 00:05:16,463
the paper. That means that you make the
same assumptions as the authors of the

92
00:05:16,483 --> 00:05:19,823
research paper, you review the
work in your head, kind of,

93
00:05:19,843 --> 00:05:22,703
and then you compare your
version with their version.

94
00:05:22,723 --> 00:05:26,943
For a study in HCI that looks something,
it looks something like you're imagining,

95
00:05:26,963 --> 00:05:28,243
you're running the study yourself.

96
00:05:28,263 --> 00:05:30,803
Who would you recruit?
What would you ask them?

97
00:05:30,823 --> 00:05:33,563
Would you measure the same thing that
the authors of this paper measure,

98
00:05:33,583 --> 00:05:36,803
you compare that answer
with what the authors do.

99
00:05:36,823 --> 00:05:41,003
Every place where your version is
different from the version in the paper is

100
00:05:41,023 --> 00:05:45,555
either a weak spot in the paper or an
idea for you for your own research work.

101
00:05:45,575 --> 00:05:49,600
And the third part takes time. According
to the person that started this method,

102
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:53,940
it can take about three, four, five
hours for beginners and about an hour for

103
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:56,800
an experienced reader. I would say
a student, based on my experience,

104
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:00,525
you should just be on the higher
side, just project like 3-4-5 hours.

105
00:06:00,545 --> 00:06:03,165
But of course you don't deep dive into
a paper like that if you haven't first

106
00:06:03,185 --> 00:06:06,685
gone through the first and the second
pass to know that it is actually relevant

107
00:06:06,705 --> 00:06:10,430
for your work. Again you don't want to
commit 5-4 hours to a paper that is not

108
00:06:10,450 --> 00:06:14,510
going to help your thesis. Now reading a
research paper critically means checking

109
00:06:14,530 --> 00:06:18,530
whether you should believe it because the
fact that a paper is published doesn't

110
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:21,950
also make it the right paper to reference
or to build your own research on.

111
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:24,650
So there are three things that I
would check on every research paper.

112
00:06:24,670 --> 00:06:28,610
The first is the sample size, which is
how many people or things that the authors

113
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:32,050
of the paper study. Bigger sample size
doesn't automatically means better.

114
00:06:32,070 --> 00:06:36,330
Keep this in mind. In HCI, for example,
small studies are very common.

115
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:41,270
Kelly Caine looked at every paper
published in 2014 and found that the most

116
00:06:41,290 --> 00:06:46,670
common sample size was 12. So 12 people
in a long interview can tell you a lot

117
00:06:46,690 --> 00:06:48,670
about how people experience something.

118
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:52,843
But 12 people can't tell you what
percentage of all students do something.

119
00:06:52,863 --> 00:06:56,222
So the question is whether the
number fits the size of the claim.

120
00:06:56,242 --> 00:06:59,322
The second thing to look out for is
the method, which is how they got their

121
00:06:59,342 --> 00:07:02,282
answer. And confirm whether
it matches the question.

122
00:07:02,302 --> 00:07:06,802
Say a research paper claims that
switching between apps stresses people.

123
00:07:06,822 --> 00:07:08,742
Did they have people in a survey?

124
00:07:08,762 --> 00:07:14,762
Did they watch people work? Did they
measure something like time or heart rates?

125
00:07:14,782 --> 00:07:18,102
Was it in a lab? or at these
people's offices and jobs,

126
00:07:18,122 --> 00:07:22,402
who were the participants. If everyone
in the study was a university student,

127
00:07:22,422 --> 00:07:27,522
I'd be very careful applying the results
of that study to say nurses on a night

128
00:07:27,542 --> 00:07:31,442
shift. The third thing I'd
look out for is who funded it.

129
00:07:31,462 --> 00:07:34,442
Look for a funding section if there
is, maybe acknowledgement or a conflict

130
00:07:34,462 --> 00:07:35,698
of interest statement.

131
00:07:36,187 --> 00:07:38,887
Funding doesn't exactly
make the study wrong.

132
00:07:38,907 --> 00:07:45,407
But in 2017, a Cochrane review looked
at drug and medical device studies and

133
00:07:45,427 --> 00:07:50,527
they found that a study sponsored by
the companies that made the product,

134
00:07:50,547 --> 00:07:54,547
we are more likely to report results or
conclusion that favored that product.

135
00:07:54,567 --> 00:07:57,427
So when the funder benefits
from one particular answer,

136
00:07:57,447 --> 00:07:59,447
you need to pay a little
bit more attention.

137
00:07:59,467 --> 00:08:02,587
And coming back to the idea of whether
printing out your research papers or

138
00:08:02,607 --> 00:08:04,807
not, how do you keep notes
on your research papers?

139
00:08:04,827 --> 00:08:06,367
Keeping notes on your
research papers matter.

140
00:08:06,387 --> 00:08:08,827
How do you even read them?
Do you read them digitally?

141
00:08:08,847 --> 00:08:12,267
I have started to print them
out, to read them physically,

142
00:08:12,287 --> 00:08:14,047
because I think that works better for me.

143
00:08:14,067 --> 00:08:16,627
There are just a few papers that are
relevant to my thesis and like they are

144
00:08:16,647 --> 00:08:20,372
really really major for me. So you might
want to print those kinds of papers

145
00:08:20,392 --> 00:08:23,552
out and just have them at hand or you
can just read them digitally whichever

146
00:08:23,572 --> 00:08:27,632
works for you. But keeping notes on the
research papers that you're using matters

147
00:08:27,652 --> 00:08:31,292
because you don't write anything down
you you'll not remember a lot of it.

148
00:08:31,312 --> 00:08:33,932
One of the tools that is always
recommended in my school is Zotero.

149
00:08:33,952 --> 00:08:38,892
Zotero is a very good research tool
and I think it is free and open source.

150
00:08:38,912 --> 00:08:41,832
You can open your PDF inside
these highlights, you know,

151
00:08:41,852 --> 00:08:43,552
write notes and it and all of that.

152
00:08:43,572 --> 00:08:47,352
But after all of this, if you think that
a research paper is relevant to your

153
00:08:47,372 --> 00:08:52,092
research topic, but it still doesn't make
sense after following all of the steps,

154
00:08:52,112 --> 00:08:53,212
then there are a few things you can do.

155
00:08:53,232 --> 00:08:56,552
You can look up some of the terms
that you don't know first of all,

156
00:08:56,572 --> 00:09:00,112
because if there are like terms, a lot
of terms in a section that you don't

157
00:09:00,132 --> 00:09:03,312
know, it can make the whole section
feel impossible to understand.

158
00:09:03,332 --> 00:09:06,872
Or you can find a survey paper on the
same topic and read it before you go back

159
00:09:06,892 --> 00:09:10,612
to the research paper. So survey paper
is kind of like a paper that reviews

160
00:09:10,632 --> 00:09:15,550
the research on a topic. Also, you can
use AI to explain like dense paragraphs.

161
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:19,170
I do this a lot. Just check what it
tells you against the paper itself,

162
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:21,390
right? Because they are
getting things wrong sometimes.

163
00:09:21,410 --> 00:09:22,840
But yeah, that's it.
